---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FitFinder from '../components/FitFinder.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'CRM Fit Finder',
  applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
  url: 'https://opsprimer.com/fit-finder/',
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Michel Modesto', url: 'https://opsprimer.com/about/' },
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="CRM Fit Finder — which CRM actually fits how you work"
  description="Five questions, a named recommendation with its weakness stated, and a link you can share. No email, no data collected."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="hero-figure">
    <b>5</b><span>questions, no email step</span>
  </div>

  <h1>Which CRM actually fits how you work</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Most comparison pages answer "which is best". That question has no answer. This one
    answers "which fits the shape you're in" — and when the honest answer is a tool I
    earn nothing from, that's what it says.
  </p>

  <FitFinder heading="Answer five questions" />

  <h2>How this decides</h2>
  <p>
    In order: anything client-facing outranks everything else, because free-tier
    branding can't be removed. Then whether email is the core of the work. Then the
    structural limits — one pipeline, two users. Then size: a one-person operation with
    a small pipeline usually loses time to a full CRM rather than saving it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every number behind those rules comes from
    <a href="/articles/hubspot-free-plan-limits/">HubSpot's official product catalog</a>,
    checked against an account I set up and ran.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
